# DECISIONS — slice S03. Append-only: one line per decision, `date · question · choice · reason · who ruled`.

The mission's gap table and the two `V-ROW: NEW` blocks live in `../S01/DECISIONS.md`; they are not
copied here, because one source or the copies drift.

| date | question | choice | reason | who ruled |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-24 | Is a stale README a slice at all, or a chore folded into S01? | Its own slice. | The kit's §11 is the operator's only instruction set for adding a vendor, and it is the document a first provision is followed from — `deploy/vps/README.md:16-17` says each stale item "is stale in a way that would stop a first provision". A chore folded into a code slice is the chore that gets dropped when the code runs late. | REQ-PES (default) |
| 2026-09-24 | Fix only the two bullets, or widen the pin as well? | Widen the pin (R3.5). | A finding is a SAMPLE of a class (protocol §3.2). The class is not "two stale bullets" — it is "the §11 refusal table is pinned against ONE constant of three codes (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:740-744`) while the file claims the two cannot drift (`:727-731`)". Fixing the text without widening the pin guarantees the third drift. | protocol §3.2 |
| 2026-09-24 | Should the widened pin retype the code list inside the test? | No — R3.5 requires the test to read the codes out of the source. | A retyped list is a third copy that drifts on its own, and the existing pin's shape (`tests/unit/v9-provider-credential-files.test.ts:400-405` slices the constant out of the source text) already shows the house answer. | REQ-PES, following the shipped pin's shape |
| 2026-09-24 | Should S03 build the probe-freshness floor while it is in §11 anyway? | No — R3.6 documents, R3.5's class does not cover it, and the floor is a `V-ROW: NEW` in `../S01/DECISIONS.md`. | The number is V's register value (`docs/architecture/05-register-skeleton.md:506`; `ADR-0015:127-131`). A seat that invents it has invented a product decision inside a documentation slice. | REQ-PES (default), routed to V |
| 2026-09-24 | Should S03 repair the three remaining known-stale bullets (`deploy/vps/README.md:28-38`)? | Re-read each against the tree; leave it or remove it, and record which in `PROGRESS.md` (R3.7). | Two of them (`:32-34` KEK rotation, `:35-38` the api.env keys) say they are already fixed in the tree but still sit in a list headed "read this before following the section" — which is itself a stale list. Removing a bullet that is false costs a line; leaving it costs an operator a wrong belief. But NONE is edited without the `path:line` that shows it false, so the slice cannot guess. | REQ-PES (default) |
| 2026-09-24 | Does S03 touch any file under `apps/` or `packages/`? | No — §2 makes it an out-of-scope statement and acceptance step 7 measures it. | The six missing codes are already emitted by shipped source; writing them into the table is a documentation change. A slice that quietly edits product code while calling itself a README refresh is the hardest kind of diff to review. | REQ-PES (default) |
| 2026-09-24 | Where does the enumeration of operator-facing refusal codes live — ONE inventory or several? (the question `PLAN.md` §5 was scaffolded to answer) | **Several, each beside the code that raises it**; the pin reads the UNION of a named anchor list written in `PLAN.md` §1b. REJECTED: (a) one new exported inventory constant in `packages/providers/src/index.ts` — the shape a reader expects, but SPEC §2 and row 6 above forbid this slice any file under `apps/` or `packages/`, and acceptance step 7 measures it; (b) a file-wide idiom scan of `packages/providers/src/index.ts` — measured, and it is a dead end: a scan of every uppercase string literal yields **59** tokens in that one file (`CLASSIFIER`, `PARSED`, `UNKNOWN`, the env-var name `PROVIDER_DISCOVERY_TARGETS_JSON`…), and a scan of the `throw new TypeError` idiom alone yields 15 codes of which **8** are absent from §11 — six more than R3.3 orders, so the pin could never go green inside this slice's scope. | The house has already chosen several, twice, with the reason written down: `packages/providers/src/index.ts:727-731` and `apps/api/src/support/model.ts:34-39` each describe an inventory owned by the surface that emits it. Six of the twelve enumerated codes have no inventory at all and are read from their function or class body; that asymmetry is the price of the `apps/`+`packages/` prohibition, not a design preference. | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | Which extraction regex does the widened pin use? | `` /[`"]([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{4,})(?=[:`"])/gu `` — both quote styles. REJECTED: the existing pin's `/"([A-Z_]+)"/gu` (`tests/unit/v9-provider-credential-files.test.ts:405`), reused by habit. | The price codes are raised from BACKTICK templates (`` `PROVIDER_TARGET_PRICE_REQUIRED:${target.providerRef}` ``, `packages/providers/src/index.ts:687`, `:700`). A double-quote-only regex yields ZERO for anchor E4 and the pin passes while pinning nothing — TOOLING-TRAPS `:3088`, a surface derived from ONE idiom misses the assertion the SPEC itself names. The regex is proved on a known-hit fixture in the same run (`probes/ARCH-PES-S03/enumeration.log`). | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | Is the enumeration bounded to START-UP refusals, or does it include the run-time spend stops? | **Start-up only.** The three run-time inventories are excluded by name: `PROVIDER_COST_ENVELOPE_REFUSAL_CODES` (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:934-946`, 4 codes, all four absent from §11), `RUN_LEVEL_SPEND_STOP_CODES` (`packages/kernel/src/index.ts:409-413`), `ENVELOPE_STOP_CODES` (`apps/runner/src/index.ts:149`). REJECTED: including them, which would add four rows R3.3 does not name and would not close R3.5 without inventing documentation for a surface the SPEC did not scope. | Every code in those three is raised DURING a run against a ceiling already reached; every one of §11's 11 existing rows and every one of R3.3's six is raised AT START-UP, and the table's heading is `What the hosted mode refuses, in code`. With the exclusion, the enumeration's absent set is character-for-character R3.3's six (measured: `probes/ARCH-PES-S03/enumeration.log` prints `CLOSES WITH SPEC R3.3's SIX? true`), so R3.3 and R3.5 close on each other with nothing left over. Routed to V below; the default binds so no node waits. | ARCH-PES-S03 (default), routed to V |
| 2026-09-24 | Is R3.5 built as ONE added test case or as an edit to the existing case at `tests/unit/v9-provider-credential-files.test.ts:397`? | **One added `it(...)`**, taking the suite from 23 to 24. REJECTED: widening the existing case in place, which leaves the pair at 23 and reads identically in prose. | Two coders would split on this exactly as REQ-REV's B1 class predicts, and the cluster command's expected pair is the only thing that can forbid the fork. A separate case is also separately nameable in `PROGRESS.md`, and it leaves the credential inventory's own case meaning what it has always meant. | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | Does the §11 worked example keep the `api.env` form as prose ("the same entry with …"), or write both JSON objects out in full? | **Both in full**, two lines, each labelled EXACT in `PLAN.md` C2-6. REJECTED: keeping the prose form and letting the `api.env` price members be carried by reference. | R3.2's own reason is "so a target pasted from §11 boots", and a sentence cannot be pasted. Acceptance step 4 also asks for a hit inside the worked example; two full lines make the count a named oracle (3 hits: the member-table row and the two example lines) instead of a judgement. | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | What price amounts does the §11 example print? | `3000000` / `15000000` micro-USD per million tokens, with one sentence saying they are the vendor's own published rate and are replaced per vendor. REJECTED: the floor `1`/`1` — a valid value that an operator pasting the block would publish, silently under-billing every call, which is the exact failure `PROVIDER_TARGET_PRICE_ZERO` exists to stop one step earlier. | The example is illustrative by construction alongside `vendor:acme` and `api.acme.example`; a round number well above the 1-micro-unit floor (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:699`) is pasteable without being mistakable for a real rate, and the sentence removes the remaining doubt. | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | Do the two clusters run in parallel? | **No — sequential, C1 then C2.** REJECTED: parallel on the grounds that their regions of `deploy/vps/README.md` are disjoint. | The single-writer rule is per FILE, not per region, and TOOLING-TRAPS `:522` records that disjoint write surfaces do not imply independent effects. C1's widened pin READS the README that C2 writes. Sequential also makes C2's anchors re-measurable after C1 lands (TOOLING-TRAPS `:3076`). | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | Where do the four document-fact cases (R3.1/R3.2, R3.4, R3.6, R3.7) live? | **`tests/unit/v9-provider-credential-files.test.ts`**, taking it 24 → 28; `tests/architecture/vps-deployment-baseline.test.ts` is left **byte-identical** at 31/31. REJECTED: (a) adding them to the kit baseline suite, the obvious home — it would report 35/35 and acceptance step 2 (`SPEC.md:128-132`) requires that suite's reported pair to be "its base pair from the intake's baseline table", 31/31, so the frozen step could not pass; (b) a third test file — satisfies step 2 too, but no acceptance step runs it. | Reading "its base pair" as "base plus what this slice adds" would be a quiet reinterpretation of a FROZEN requirement, which `heartbeat-architecture` §1 forbids; the fix is a different file, not a different reading. Step 2 pins no number for `v9` ("Both report every case passing"), so `v9` may grow. The choice also makes R3.8's answer trivial and checkable — `git diff --stat origin/dev...HEAD -- tests/architecture/` prints nothing, so zero assertions changed — and step 2's single command exercises every case the slice adds. | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | Is an ADR written? | **No.** The candidate rule — "a new operator-facing refusal code must appear in §11 and in an inventory the pin reads" — outlives the mission, but it is enforced by the widened pin itself. Next free number measured at PLAN time: **ADR-0025**. | An ADR restating a live test is a second copy that drifts, which is the exact defect R3.5 exists to close. The number is recorded so a later node does not re-measure `ls docs/architecture/01-decisions/`. | ARCH-PES-S03 |
| 2026-09-24 | R3.7's three judgement bullets — leave or remove, each measured against the tree? | **B3 `:28-31` REMOVE** (C2-7 deletes the sentence it calls stale, so the bullet describes a staleness that no longer exists); **B4 `:32-34` LEAVE** (§10 still carries "KEK rotation is not implemented" at `deploy/vps/README.md:703`, so the bullet is true); **B5 `:35-38` LEAVE** (both keys are present at `deploy/vps/env/api.env.example:35` and `:57`, and §3's layout table at `deploy/vps/README.md:148-167` still lists neither `support-kek.bin` nor a support database principal, so both halves are true). | R3.7 forbids editing a bullet without the `path:line` that shows it false. Each verdict above carries that line, measured in the lane at PLAN time; the BUILD seat re-measures and records the same three in `PROGRESS.md`. B3's removal depends on C2-7, which is why C2-9 runs after it. | ARCH-PES-S03 |

## `V-ROW: NEW` blocks raised by ARCH-PES-S03

These are ARCH's, distinct from the two that live in `../S01/DECISIONS.md`. Neither is numbered here;
the orchestrator numbers them at transcription into the V packet. Both defaults are applied, so no
node waits on a ruling.

**`V-ROW: NEW` · S03 · ARCH t_21a1edcf · the run-time spend codes.**
`packages/providers/src/index.ts:934-946` exports `PROVIDER_COST_ENVELOPE_REFUSAL_CODES` — four codes
(`RUN_COST_ENVELOPE_MONEY_REACHED`, `PROVIDER_USAGE_UNREPORTED`, `COST_ENVELOPE_CHARGE_UNREPRESENTABLE`,
`DAILY_COST_ENVELOPE_REACHED`), **all four absent from §11** and pinned against it by nothing. They
are raised during a run, not at boot.
**Recommended default:** leave them out of §11's refusal table and out of the widened pin. The table's
heading is `What the hosted mode refuses, in code` and all 17 of its rows after this slice are
start-up refusals; a run-time spend stop in that table sends an operator looking for a boot problem
that is not there. Documenting them is its own slice, in its own section, with its own operator action.
**Smallest yes/no for V:** "Keep §11's refusal table to start-up refusals only, and leave the four
run-time spend codes for a later section?"
**VERDICT** exclude / **CONFIDENCE** medium / **STRONGEST COUNTER:** R3.5's own words are "every
operator-facing refusal code the provider, credential, price and **cost-envelope** surface can emit",
and `PROVIDER_COST_ENVELOPE_REFUSAL_CODES` is literally the cost-envelope surface's inventory. On that
reading the exclusion is the one-idiom defect TOOLING-TRAPS `:3088` names, and an operator whose run
died on `DAILY_COST_ENVELOPE_REACHED` will search this runbook for the code and find nothing.

*(A second V row was drafted here and then withdrawn: acceptance step 2's "its base pair" looked
like it contradicted R3.8 once C2's cases were placed in the kit suite. It does not contradict
anything once the cases are placed in `v9` instead. The row is recorded as a decision above rather
than sent to V, because the SPEC needed no reinterpretation — only a different file.)*

