## Revision 2 — ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 (ARCH-FIX(S03) pass 2, ticket `t_2913aad6`), from `reviews/ARCH-REV-S03-p1.md`

Appended; nothing above is rewritten. Every measurement cited lives under
`.hermes/reports/provider-env-selection/probes/ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2/`, and each checker there was
first watched FAILING on one planted false expectation (`*-selftest.log`) before its pass was quoted.

| date | question | choice | reason | who ruled |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-24 | B1 — how is a number in a done-criterion derived? | It is the OUTPUT of the step's literal command, RUN on a simulated correct edit at that step's own boundary, and run again on a mutant that must change it. Never an arithmetic sum. Table lines are counted with `/usr/bin/grep -c '^\|'` over a `sed` text-anchored span, so pass 1's numbers **19** (refusal) and **8** (member) and their member lists stand; only the pattern changed. REJECTED: (a) keep pass 1's `'^\| '` and correct the numbers to 18 and 7 — that pattern skips the separator only because it is spelled `\|---\|---\|`, so a `\| --- \| --- \|` separator would move the count; (b) count data rows only with `'^\| \`'` (17 and 6) — exact, but it changes every number and member list and assumes every first cell is a code span. | ARCH-REV p1 B1: pass 1 wrote 19 and 8 as sums (header + separator + rows), never ran the pattern, and the pattern excluded the separator. Measured (`oracles.log`): base 13 and 6; a correct C1-2 gives 19, a correct C2-5 gives 8; a five-row mutant gives 18, a one-row mutant 7; pass 1's `'^\| '` gives 18 and 7 on the correct edits. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | B1.3 — what replaces C2-6's whole-file `input_price_micros_per_million` count? | Two per-line position commands: one worked-example line per env file carrying BOTH price keys in JSON-key form, each printing `1`. REJECTED: the minimal fix, 3 → 4, which re-creates the class it closes. Measured (`oracles.log`, B1.3): C2-5's Value cells written as variant B ("declaring it without `input_price_micros_per_million` refuses…", the phrasing C2-5's own instruction invites) make the file count 5 at C2-6's boundary, so 4 condemns a correct edit. | A count of a name that PROSE may repeat is not an oracle. The per-line check is prose-independent (1 and 1 on both variants), does not depend on C2-9 having run, and checks what R3.2 actually says: both members, in both forms. The old count looked only at `input…`. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | B1 — how many members does the class have? ARCH-REV p1's table lists three. | **Seven fixed, ten checked correct and left alone.** The class: a done-criterion whose written output is not what a correct state produces at that step's boundary, so that a stranger cannot mark the step done. Fixed: the three named (C1-2, C2-5, C2-6), plus four this sweep found. (4) C1-1 ran `run-suites.sh …v9…:23:1` and expected `CLUSTER_RED`; the runner prints GREEN when every pair equals its expectation (`run-suites.sh:23`, `:25`), so a correct RED state printed GREEN. (5) C2-5 stated its base as "6 lines beginning `\| `", which is 5 under that pattern. (6) The §4 acceptance row for step 4 said "3 lines", a prose-dependent total. (7) C2-5's "C2-1's member-table half passes" could only be judged by reading which assertion failed; it is now two first-cell commands. Checked and unchanged: C1-1's `union.size` 12; the RED pairs 24:1…24:4 of C2-1…C2-4; C1-4 and C2-10's pairs; C2-3's and C2-8's single `max_tokens`; C2-9's 2; the §4 rows for steps 3, 5 and 6. | The packet warns that a charge which pins a count from a finding's table propagates that table's omissions. Every oracle was measured, member by member, in `oracles.log` and `simulate.log`. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | N1 — where does C1-3's RED case live? | INSIDE C1-1's one case, as assertion (3), after the 12-code loop, and it reads only the text BELOW the table's last `\|` line. REJECTED: (a) a separate `it(...)` — the reviewer allowed it ("in the C1-1 case or beside it"), but it moves every downstream pair (C1 24 → 25, C2 28 → 29) and three done-when numbers with it; (b) a check over the whole refusal span — measured VACUOUS: it passes the moment C1-2 lands, because the `…PRICE_INVALID` row's own Meaning cell says "before any hosted rule runs" (`simulate.log`, "NAIVE whole-span guard @S1"). | Inside the case, no pair moves, and the RED message at each boundary is fixed by the assertion order: `PROVIDER_TARGET_PRICE_REQUIRED` at base; `guard-order sentence below the table: PROVIDER_DISCOVERY_TARGET_PRICE_INVALID` after C1-2; PASS after C1-3. That was measured by executing the plan's own code block (`plan-code.log`). | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | N4 — add a guard for the `<ref>` house style, or only stop crediting `:418` and `:378`? | Both. C1-1's assertion (1) forbids `/<[a-z-]+>/u` anywhere in the refusal span, and the false credit is removed. Because (1) never fails on a correct build, C1-1 carries a mutant check at its own boundary (insert one `<ref>` row, watch (1) fail, delete that line, confirm `git diff --stat` is empty). REJECTED: removing the credit alone, which leaves a BINDING rule with no guard — the N1 shape. Class swept, a guard credited to a test that does not implement it: C2-6 also credited `:378` with "neither contains `<`" (corrected), and the R3.9 trace had C1-2 claiming R3.9 without a fence clause and C2-8 claiming it with no supporting text. R3.9 is now traced to C1-4, C2-6, C2-8 and C2-10, and C2-8 carries the fence clause. | `:418` forbids one string; `:378` inspects ```` ```sh ```` blocks only (both re-measured by content, `consistency.log`). The reverse trace was rewritten as ONE line: pass 1's wrapped form parsed as ten orphan steps in ARCH-REV p1's first parser (`measure.log` §7). Both reviewer parsers now agree, with zero gaps (`reviewer-trace-at-rev2.log`, `reviewer-measure-at-rev2.log`). | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | N2 — C2-3's cost-paragraph oracle | `\b600_?000\b`. Strip the phrase `no recommended value`, then ban `\brecommend\w*`, `\bsuggest\w*` and `\bshould be\b`. C2-8 states the same four wording rules, so a seat who follows C2-8 passes C2-3. REJECTED: the reviewer's WHEN implemented literally (whole words, both spellings). Measured on seven fixtures (`simulate.log`, N2), it PASSES "The recommended value is 600000." (`\brecommend\b` does not match `recommended`) and `6000000` (a substring test finds `600000` inside it). Those are the two violations the check exists to catch. | The chosen oracle passes all three faithful spellings the finding names (`600000`, `600_000`, R3.6's own sentence) and fails all four violations. Pass 1's oracle failed two faithful spellings. The whole-word oracle passes two violations. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | N3 — the rejected `throw new TypeError` scan of `packages/providers/src/index.ts`: the count row "enumeration" above records as 15 codes, 8 absent | **Re-measured: 16 distinct, 9 absent from §11** (`scan.log`), matching ARCH-REV p1. Pass 1 was wrong twice, for separate reasons. (i) Its grep is line-based and cannot see a `throw new TypeError(` whose code sits on the next line — `packages/providers/src/index.ts:786-787` (`PROVIDER_AUTHORIZATION_FILE_UNUSABLE`). It was watched missing that on a two-throw fixture first. (ii) "8" was a transcription error: pass 1's own inline output printed NINE `ABSENT` lines and they were counted by eye. The "59 uppercase tokens" figure in the same row re-runs as 59 with its own command. | The 16/9 counts come from a whole-text regex; both are counted by `wc -l`, not by eye. After this slice six of the nine stay absent: the four V-9 codes, plus the two rows below. PLAN §1b now scopes "no leftover" to the eight anchors and names all ten codes outside them. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | N3 — `CONFIGURED_PROVIDER_DUPLICATE` (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:255`): in the table, or outside? | Outside, recorded, no V row. It is not reached through §11's procedure: step 4 publishes through `buildConfiguredProviderSetDeploymentRow`, whose shape check refuses a duplicate ref at publish (`packages/register/src/configured-provider-set.ts:155-162`), so the boot throw is a second guard behind that one. REJECTED: a seventh row, which widens R3.3's six with no SPEC basis. STRONGEST COUNTER: an operator who writes the configured-set row by hand, outside `publishGeneral`, meets it at boot with no runbook entry. | The same shape as `apps/runner/src/main.ts:117` behind `:87`: a guard that can be reached only past another one. Measured by content (`consistency.log`). | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | N3 — `PROVIDER_LENGTH_RETRY_FAILURE_COUNT_INVALID` (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:1045`) | Outside, recorded, no V row: not operator-facing. It guards an argument of `lengthRetryTokenCeiling`, whose only caller (`:1151`) passes an internal retry counter. | R3.5 bounds the class to OPERATOR-FACING codes; no configuration an operator writes reaches this one. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
| 2026-09-24 | C1-3's sentence: free wording or EXACT? | EXACT, and narrowed to the measured order only: parse → `assertDeploymentProviderTargets` → `assertPricedProviderTargets`. REJECTED: pass 1's wording ("then the hosted rules in the rows above"), which implied an order for rows such as `DEPLOYMENT_MODE_*` that the environment loader raises before any target is parsed. | One build: the oracle's three tokens are guaranteed by the text, and the text claims nothing that was not measured. | ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p2 |
