## `V-ROW: NEW` block raised by ARCH-FIX-PES-S03-p3

Not numbered here; the orchestrator numbers it when it transcribes it into the V packet. Its default
is applied, so no node waits on a ruling.

**`V-ROW: NEW` · S03 · ARCH-FIX t_f54b7505 · the code R3.4's sentence names.**
The docstring of `readSealedCostEnvelopeStatus` (`packages/register/src/runtime-environment.ts:105-128`)
says that with the shipped source `COST_ENVELOPES_NOT_SEALED` "is unreachable at runtime" (`:112-114`),
because the seal reads a frozen in-source row. It adds that "THE LIVE FAIL-CLOSED GATE IS ELSEWHERE"
(`:118-124`): a hosted deployment whose `REGISTER_VERSION` has no `costEnvelopePolicy` row refuses
with `COST_ENVELOPE_POLICY_UNRESOLVED`, and one whose row is malformed refuses with
`COST_ENVELOPE_POLICY_INVALID`. The API reads that row at `apps/api/src/main.ts:241-242` and the runner
at `apps/runner/src/main.ts:111`. SPEC R3.4 (frozen) has C2-7 write that "a hosted deployment refuses
to start until the cost envelopes are sealed, with the code `COST_ENVELOPES_NOT_SEALED`". An operator
who follows that sentence waits for a code the shipped build never emits. The refusal-table row at
`deploy/vps/README.md:779` says the same thing and predates this slice.
**Recommended default** (it applies until V rules, because a seat cannot move a frozen SPEC): R3.4
stands, and C2-7 writes the SPEC's sentence unchanged. C1-2 adds `COST_ENVELOPE_POLICY_UNRESOLVED` and
`COST_ENVELOPE_POLICY_INVALID` to the same table, so the live codes are in §11 whichever way V rules.
**Smallest yes/no for V:** "Amend R3.4 so that §11's support-chat sentence names
`COST_ENVELOPE_POLICY_UNRESOLVED` / `COST_ENVELOPE_POLICY_INVALID` as the refusal a hosted operator
meets, and calls `COST_ENVELOPES_NOT_SEALED` what it is: a check on the integrity of the build?"
**VERDICT:** yes, amend: a SPEC v2 of R3.4, and one sentence in C2-7 / **CONFIDENCE:** medium /
**STRONGEST COUNTER:** R3.4 copies the README's own known-stale bullet B3 (`:28-31`) and the row at
`:779`, and the seal does fire on a build whose constant is broken, so the sentence is true of some
build. The six new rows also name the live codes in the same section, a few lines away.

